{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/581868-reflecting-many-fetches-as-group-antagonists","id":581868,"title":"Reflecting Many-Fetches as Group Antagonists","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-07-06T20:08:21-07:00","id":581868,"title":"Reflecting Many-Fetches as Group Antagonists","content":"Question of the day: Fetches are geared towards their changeling in pretty much all ways. This helps build up the intimacy of the horror and the conflict with a given character, but it's got some problems as a way of engaging the whole group. So, our question is simple-how can any given fetch be turned into a conflict for a group of PC's, and how can you build them up as a center figure for larger problem(story arcs, at the least)?<br /> <br /> It's worth noting that if the True Fae has himself, Huntsmen, Loyalists and occasionally privateers, and their cadre of hobgoblins as pieces on the board, fetches seem to fill the role of pawn-build the line, hold the line.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-06T22:47:53-07:00","id":581971,"title":"","content":"Hmm... A couple of possibilities occur to me, though I'm unsure how full they would necessarily prove. They mostly stem from an important mortal friend/ally/contact being taken and replaced, someone who would give the motley pause on considering just trying to eliminate the thing. Perhaps the mortal was simply too pivotal a contact. Perhaps the &quot;death&quot; would cause too much pain to someone whom members of the motley care about deeply. Perhaps there is the possibility of a rescue in the relatively near-future, and &quot;death&quot; would complicate the rescued resuming her old life. Perhaps the motley simply aren't the types to comfortably kill a Fetch.<br /> <br /> 1) The Fetch may be at least as helpful to the motley, but sometimes engages in self-destructive behavior. Maybe getting in with some criminal element (drugs, gambling debts, etc.) or the like. Whatever the case, this creates some big problems for everything it touches. The motley gets drawn in, forced to either clean up the messes created or heading them off at the pass, keeping the Fetch from running the life it's stolen into the ground. And keeping other Changelings from deciding to take matters into their own hands, if the Fetch creates enough waves.<br /> <br /> 2) The Fetch is unusually aware of what it is, perhaps even an agent of the Stranger who left it. The Fetch may broker deals with the motley to continue its prior arrangements (or &quot;arrangements&quot;), twisting whatever leverage it can. Maybe on behalf of one of the Gentry, maybe to its own benefit, or maybe drawn into the web of someone or something else -- or some combination thereof. The motley may never have realized just how much the one who was taken meant, until the Fetch stepped in to make their lives a living hell or extort them to the last drop. Secrets of theirs known to only that person, pledges the Changelings had sworn generously as shows of trust, connections to those similarly close to the motley, or services they suddenly realized they couldn't live without are all fair game.<br /> <br /> 3) Okay, I lied. This one doesn't depend on someone the PCs know. They aren't dealing with such a Fetch as either of the above, but <i>another</i> motley isn't so lucky. This other group of Changelings unwillingly find themselves at cross purposes to the PCs, and no doubt hating that they've been reduced to tools in such a fashion. It's an indirect challenge, especially if the Changelings are forced to take great pains to keep the PCs from finding out the Fetch's identity.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://avatarcomic.net/exalted-wiki/Exalted_3E:What_We_Know\" target=\"_blank\">The EX3 What We Know Wiki</a>, courtesy of Marin and JMobius<br /> <a href=\"http://nishkriya.com\" target=\"_blank\">Nishkriya, the EX3 Freelancer Quotes Tracker</a>, courtesy of Anathema and Moonwolf<br /> <a href=\"http://forums.whitewolfarchive.com/\" target=\"_blank\">The White Wolf Forum Archive</a>, courtesy of The Dark Wizard and Sanctaphrax (see <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/PhilippeSaner?u=159709\" target=\"_blank\">its Patreon</a>) <hr />Arrghus: There's also the Shepherd of the North Star, busily guiding the one thing in all the heavens that <i>doesn't move</i>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/558-gaius","name":"Gaius","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2028"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-07T11:59:44-07:00","id":582598,"title":"","content":"As I recall, in addition to developing Echoes, Fetches can go a little bit <i>strange</i>, psychologically, when confronted with their Changeling double. <i>Autumn Nightmares</i> gives the example of Bobby Slim, a Fetch rejected by his parents because they recognized that he was not their son, and who ultimately becomes a serial killer partly as a result of premonitions he has that his Changeling is coming for him. A Fetch whose artificial mind breaks as a result of being confronted by the reality of their own condition could become an unpredictable threat to everyone around them, and it makes sense that their Changeling counterpart might recruit her motley-mates as a posse to go after them.<br /> <br /> ...And now I suddenly want to write a Changeling story based on <i>High Noon</i>...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-07T19:07:57-07:00","id":583008,"title":"","content":"Unlike Changelings most Fetches have little to fear from mortal scrutiny, calling the cops is often a good response to Changeling advances. They can also take a pro-active stance towards Changelings, plotting out attacks using mortal authorities, all it takes is a few anonymous tips to the police or even media to create a world of headaches for the local freehold.<br /> Why can't Fetches make motleys of their own?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-07T22:40:18-07:00","id":583208,"title":"","content":"A Fetch who fights smart can easily be a worthy adversary for a whole motley. They can lure the players into a trap or strike from the shadows and then vanish in an instant (especially effective with Normalcy and Shadow Step). Or it can send others (hired thugs, other enemies of the motley, or creatures created with Craft Fetch-Beast) to do its dirty work. If the players want to take the fight to the Fetch, maybe its gone off the grid and finding it could be an entire story. Or maybe they know that it has the Call the Fae Echo, so they have to be very careful about attacking it. When they finally do kill it, maybe it just comes back at them a few days later like nothing happened (because it had Craft Second Fetch). On top of that, maybe it has dangerous allies. It could have made deals with creatures in the Hedge, or even members of the players' Freehold (who may have thought they were making a deal with a Changeling thanks to False Seeming). The main book suggests that the fetch could be someone with wealth or a member of a criminal organization, which are certainly good options. The Fetch could also have insinuated itself in a group of Hunters that the Fetch can direct at the players (in fact, I think this would be pretty likely in an area with a lot of Changelings, given how Attuned to the Wyrd works). If you're using other supernaturals, perhaps the Fetch has become fairly knowledgeable about Changelings and the Fae and has bartered that knowledge for favors from a Mage (or Werewolf, or Vampire, or whatever - Mages just seem like the most likely choice). All of those options can also be useful in situations where the players need to find the Fetch or convince it to help for plot reasons (information or a McGuffin that the Fetch has, maybe), but it doesn't want to be found or is wary of the player characters.<br /> <br /> Specific Fetch story ideas:<br /> <br /> A powerful Fetch that outlived its Changeling has found some way (an unusual Echo or rare Token, perhaps) to identify other Fetches and is forming a network for mutual protection. Fetches in the network take lots of precautions; they carry guns and other weapons, take self-defense courses, share housing when possible, have extensive home security systems, keep cold iron on hand. They've also made deals with the local Goblin Market and have access to some Tokens and Hedgefruits. They even have a Hollow that belonged to a Changeling that they killed. They are aware of at least one of the local Freehold's regular meeting places and keep an eye on it. At least one member of the network is in local law enforcement. When they suspect a member of the network is being targeted by a Changeling(s), they may help the Fetch in question disappear, or, if the Fetch being targeted doesn't want to run and hide, they may set up a trap with the Fetch as bait and spring an ambush on the unsuspecting Changeling(s) who try to kill it. Once they've killed a couple of Changelings (or failed to kill one) who came gunning for their Fetches, the Freehold takes an interest. The players are tasked with investigating and what they initially think is one dangerous Fetch turns out to be a group of well-organized, very paranoid Fetches. Finding out who's behind this organization could prove very difficutl. Reaching a truce with the Fetch network may be possible, but it's likely to be political suicide in the Freehold. Attacking the network may be extremely dangerous and is likely to provoke retaliation. Once the network realizes that Changelings are aware of their activities, they'll go into crisis mode and be even more cautious. They might even go on the offensive.<br /> <br /> A team of Cheiron Group retrieval agents is using a Fetch to help them identify and acquire Changelings. Their first target may have been the Fetch's Changeling. Once enough Changelings go missing that it's obvious someone is hunting them, the players are asked by the Freehold to investigate. After they've found the retrieval agents, they'll have to decide how to handle them. Convincing the hunters that their walking Changeling detector is really a monster made of junk food wrappers and shadow won't do much good; they'd probably just keep using its services until they have no more use for it and then hand it over to their higher ups for experiments. The players might be able to convince the Fetch that it's confused, that they mean it no harm and the Cheiron Group are the real monsters. Or they could just try to kill the hunters and Fetch. If they opt to solve the problem with violence right off the bat, they learn the hard way that the Fetch has learned Death of Glamour and the Cheiron Group agents are very well armed and trained.<br /> <br /> One of the Court rulers has gone missing. After investigating, the players learn that the Court ruler had a meeting with someone on a certain day every year, which corresponds to the day they went missing. More digging uncovers that it was the ruler's Fetch, with whom the Court ruler had reached a sort of non-aggression agreement. Every year they would meet to renew the Pledge that formalized their truce. This year, something went wrong. The players have to find out where the meeting was to take place and how to combat a Fetch that likely has a very high Wyrd rating and a lot of experience. They may also want to uncover why the Fetch turned on their doppleganger, and what they intend to do with them. The meeting took place at a secluded location, which is now guarded by a veritable army of fetch-beasts and some human minions of the Fetch. The Fetch managed to use Mirror Trap on the Court ruler, and is waiting for the Gentry that created it to arrive and claim its prize. The Gentry has promised the Fetch that it will make it into a real human by granting it the rest of the Court Ruler's soul when it is done torturing its errant Changeling to death (whether it can actually do that or not is, of course, up to the ST).<br /> <br /> I've got more. Maybe later.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5910-csschulz","name":"CSSchulz","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5910_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-07-09T07:06:03-07:00","id":584684,"title":"","content":"Currently one of the fetches of my group is leading a violent prison gang from the prison. The gang is actually involved in something that interests the whole group and they all want to somehow deal with it. In this particular case the fetch always has been aware of his nature and the gang involves a couple of privateers and hobgoblins and he uses dream travel to communicate with the members, but even a group of mortal gang members with solid connections with organized criminal organizations and their potential supernatural backing can pose a serious challenge. My players are currently thinking very hard about how they are going to get at that fetch. Maximum security prison is not exactly easy to enter and they (rightfully) suspect that the one maximum military security prison in the USA will have at least some measures against the supernatural...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4560-madfox11","name":"Madfox11","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"172"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}